{
 "language": "ar",
 "title": "Arabic grammar",
 "intro": "A working reference for Modern Standard Arabic (الفصحى), the Arabic of news, books and subtitles, from beginner to intermediate. Arabic is written right to left, usually without short vowels, and builds words from three-letter roots: read a section, notice the shape, then listen for it in what you watch and read.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "number": 1,
   "id": "script",
   "title": "The script & short vowels",
   "kind": "structure",
   "level": "A1",
   "tags": [
    "script",
    "alphabet",
    "harakat",
    "vowels",
    "fatha",
    "kasra",
    "damma",
    "sukun",
    "shadda",
    "right to left"
   ],
   "rule": null,
   "text": "Arabic has 28 letters, written right to left, most joined to the next letter. Long vowels are letters (**ا** ā, **و** ū, **ي** ī); short vowels are small marks that are usually **left out** in normal text, so readers rely on knowing the word. Lingo OS looks words up without the marks, so كَتَبَ and كتب find the same entry.",
   "tables": [
    {
     "head": [
      "Mark",
      "Sound",
      "Example"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "fatha  َ",
       "a",
       "كَتَبَ | kataba (he wrote)"
      ],
      [
       "kasra  ِ",
       "i",
       "مِن | min (from)"
      ],
      [
       "damma  ُ",
       "u",
       "كُتُب | kutub (books)"
      ],
      [
       "sukun  ْ",
       "no vowel",
       "بَيْت | bayt (house)"
      ],
      [
       "shadda  ّ",
       "doubled consonant",
       "مُدَرِّس | mudarris (teacher)"
      ]
     ]
    }
   ],
   "after": null,
   "examples": [
    {
     "text": "هذا كتاب جديد.",
     "translation": "This is a new book.",
     "note": null,
     "reading": null
    },
    {
     "text": "هَذَا كِتَابٌ جَدِيدٌ.",
     "translation": "This is a new book.",
     "note": "the same sentence with all vowel marks",
     "reading": null
    }
   ],
   "points": [],
   "tip": null
  },
  {
   "number": 2,
   "id": "article",
   "title": "The article ال & sun letters",
   "kind": "structure",
   "level": "A1",
   "tags": [
    "article",
    "al",
    "ال",
    "definite",
    "sun letters",
    "moon letters",
    "assimilation"
   ],
   "rule": null,
   "text": "**ال** is \"the\" for every gender and number, written joined to its noun. Before \"sun letters\" (t, th, d, dh, r, z, s, sh, ṣ, ḍ, ṭ, ẓ, l, n) the l is not pronounced and the next consonant doubles. There is no word for \"a\": a noun without ال is indefinite.",
   "tables": [
    {
     "head": [
      "",
      "Written",
      "Said"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "moon letter (ق، ك، ب…)",
       "القمر | the moon",
       "al-qamar"
      ],
      [
       "sun letter (ش، س، ت، د، ر، ن…)",
       "الشمس | the sun",
       "ash-shams"
      ]
     ]
    }
   ],
   "after": null,
   "examples": [
    {
     "text": "البيت كبير.",
     "translation": "The house is big.",
     "note": null,
     "reading": null
    },
    {
     "text": "في السوق ناس كثيرون.",
     "translation": "There are many people in the market.",
     "note": "as-sūq: sun letter",
     "reading": null
    }
   ],
   "points": [],
   "tip": null
  },
  {
   "number": 3,
   "id": "gender",
   "title": "Gender & ة",
   "kind": "structure",
   "level": "A1",
   "tags": [
    "gender",
    "feminine",
    "ta marbuta",
    "ة",
    "masculine",
    "adjective agreement"
   ],
   "rule": null,
   "text": "Nouns are masculine or feminine; most feminine nouns end in **ة** (tā' marbūṭa, pronounced -a, or -at before another word). Adjectives follow the noun and agree in gender, number and definiteness: **بيت كبير** (a big house), **البيت الكبير** (the big house), **مدينة كبيرة** (a big city). Plurals of things take feminine singular adjectives: **سيارات جديدة** (new cars).",
   "tables": [
    {
     "head": [
      "Masculine",
      "Feminine"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "طالب | (male) student",
       "طالب{ة} | (female) student"
      ],
      [
       "كبير | big",
       "كبير{ة}"
      ],
      [
       "مدرس | teacher",
       "مدرس{ة}"
      ]
     ]
    }
   ],
   "after": null,
   "examples": [
    {
     "text": "هذه مدينة جميلة.",
     "translation": "This is a beautiful city.",
     "note": null,
     "reading": null
    },
    {
     "text": "الطالبة الجديدة من مصر.",
     "translation": "The new (female) student is from Egypt.",
     "note": null,
     "reading": null
    },
    {
     "text": "عندي سيارات قديمة.",
     "translation": "I have old cars.",
     "note": "non-human plural → feminine singular",
     "reading": null
    }
   ],
   "points": [],
   "tip": null
  },
  {
   "number": 4,
   "id": "nominal-sentence",
   "title": "Sentences without a verb (is / are)",
   "kind": "structure",
   "level": "A1",
   "tags": [
    "nominal sentence",
    "is",
    "to be",
    "هذا",
    "هذه",
    "جملة اسمية",
    "كان"
   ],
   "rule": null,
   "text": "In the present, \"is/are\" isn't said: **البيت كبير** (the house [is] big), **أنا طالب** (I [am a] student). The subject is definite, the description indefinite; compare **البيت الكبير** (the big house, a phrase). For the past or future, use **كان / يكون**: **كان البيت كبيرا**.",
   "tables": [],
   "after": null,
   "examples": [
    {
     "text": "أنا من المغرب.",
     "translation": "I'm from Morocco.",
     "note": null,
     "reading": null
    },
    {
     "text": "الجو جميل اليوم.",
     "translation": "The weather is nice today.",
     "note": null,
     "reading": null
    },
    {
     "text": "كان المطعم مزدحما.",
     "translation": "The restaurant was crowded.",
     "note": "كان for the past",
     "reading": null
    }
   ],
   "points": [],
   "tip": null
